  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Kamal Haneef vsFoodInspector on 18 January, 2016.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Kamal Haneef vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 18Th Day Of January 2016/28Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1887 of 2010 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 746/2007 Of The Additional Sessions Court (Special), Kottayam Cc 337/2004 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Changanacherry Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused: --------------------------------------- 1. Kamal Haneef, Production Manager, Penta Aqua Minerals, Plakkapady, Kangazha P.O. 2. Shibila Beevi, Chamakalayil, Kangazha P.O. By Adv. Sri.N.A.Muraleedharan Respondents/Respondents/Complainant & State: ------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Kanjirapppally Circle, Changanacherry. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Proseucutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 18-01-2016 , The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Kamal Haneef vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/149830536/ 1 Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No. 1887 of 2010 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 18th day of January, 2016",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 24.11.2000 at about 10.30 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 9 bottles of mineral water from the first accused. The second accused was the licencee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst submitted the report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for mineral water under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P23 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for mineral water under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules as it contained mercury content more than the maximum prescribed limit. It was further stated in the certificate that the ultraviolet treatment had been done before packing. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P23 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P23 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. Kamal Haneef vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/149830536/ 2 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P23 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P23 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. Kamal Haneef vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/149830536/ 2 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P23 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
